 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, the haze in Singapore is currently bad**, with air quality hovering in the **Unhealthy** to **Very Unhealthy** range. \n\nAlthough conditions eased slightly overnight compared to the previous day's peaks (when three regions exceeded a PSI of 200), the air quality remains poor:\n\n* **Morning Readings:** The National Environment Agency (NEA) recorded the **24-hour PSI between 145 and 194** (Unhealthy range) and **1-hour PM2.5 readings between 80 and 114 µg/m³** (Band 2 / Elevated).\n* **24-Hour Forecast:** The NEA forecasts the 24-hour PSI to range from **150 to 230**, spanning the **Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy** categories.\n* **Weather & Outlook:** Thundery showers are forecast over Singapore and surrounding regions, and winds are shifting to blow from the southeast or south-southeast. While this is expected to reduce the transport of smoke haze from Kalimantan, some haze from Sumatra may still drift toward the island. Air quality is expected to improve further on Sunday and Monday.\n\n**Health & Activity Advisories:**\n* **Check 1-Hour PM2.5:** The Ministry of Health (MOH) and NEA advise using the **1-hour PM2.5 readings** to decide on immediate outdoor activities, while using the 24-hour PSI forecast to plan next-day activities.\n* **Precautions:** Residents—especially the elderly, pregnant women, children, and those with chronic lung or heart conditions—should minimize prolonged or strenuous outdoor physical exertion. Some outdoor ActiveSG sports facilities and events (such as the Great Eastern Women's Run) have been closed or cancelled as a precaution.\n* **Live Updates:** You can track real-time regional readings on the official NEA haze portal ([haze.gov.sg](https://www.haze.gov.sg)) or through the **myENV** mobile app. Eligible residents looking for N95 masks can check availability at vending machines via the **HazeMaskGoWhere** website.",
